Diabetic Retinopathy
Opthalmology & ENT
Microaneurysms, Dot-and-blot hemorrhages, Neovascularization/Exudates

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-018
Reason for Visit: Acute, sudden onset of a dense swarm of floaters followed by painless blurring of central vision.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a poorly controlled diabetic patient experiencing a sudden, painless "shower of floaters" and immediate decline in visual acuity. This presentation highly suspects an acute preretinal or vitreous hemorrhage secondary to proliferative diabetic retinopathy. Urgent home assessment was mandated to rule out an acute retinal detachment (a time-critical surgical emergency) and to restrict the patient's physical activity to prevent further massive intraocular hemorrhage before specialist review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It was like a swarm of black bugs suddenly filled my right eye, and now everything is covered in a blurry, dark web."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute, sudden, and painless onset of a shower of dark floaters in the right eye occurring 4 hours ago after a coughing fit. This rapidly progressed to a significant blurring of central vision. Denies flashing lights, a peripheral "curtain" shadow, or ocular pain. Patient has a 15-year history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus with notoriously poor glycemic control and admits to missing their last two annual diabetic eye screening appointments. Asymptomatic visual progression prior to this acute event.
Functional Status: Currently immobile, resting in a chair, fearful of completely losing vision.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely anxious; spouse providing full support.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 | HR: 84 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 95 Kg | BGL: 18.4 mmol/L
General: Anxious, keeping the right eye closed.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity in the right eye is reduced to 6/60 (unable to improve with pinhole). Left eye is 6/12. Pupils are equal and reactive; no relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD). Direct ophthalmoscopy of the left eye reveals classic background diabetic changes: microaneurysms, deep dot-and-blot hemorrhages, and waxy yellow hard exudates. Ophthalmoscopy of the affected right eye yields a severely obscured fundus view with a dull red reflex, consistent with acute vitreous hemorrhage blocking the visual axis. Confrontation visual fields are difficult to formally assess on the right due to the central obscuration, but peripheral light perception remains intact.
Environmental Observations: Multiple sugary snacks visible; insulin pens left unrefrigerated on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Non-compliant with prescribed basal-bolus insulin regimen.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Proliferative Diabetic Retinopathy with Acute Vitreous Hemorrhage - Status: Acute Complication
Differentials:

Retinal Detachment - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of preceding photopsia (flashing lights) and the lack of a distinct, dense peripheral visual field defect (curtain effect), though definitive exclusion requires formal specialist B-scan ultrasonography.

Posterior Vitreous Detachment (Uncomplicated) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound reduction in visual acuity and the density of the visual obscuration (blood), which far exceeds the typical benign, translucent floaters of a simple PVD.

Central Retinal Vein Occlusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sudden "shower of floaters" symptom which specifically indicates bleeding freely into the vitreous cavity, rather than the diffuse, contained retinal hemorrhages of a CRVO.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Poor health literacy, severe acute complication)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a classic history for acute vitreous hemorrhage secondary to advanced, previously unmonitored proliferative diabetic retinopathy. Urgent face-to-face assessment was critical to identify the severe intraocular bleed, rule out the absolute emergency of acute retinal detachment (evidenced by intact peripheral light perception and lack of photopsia), and manage the immediate systemic crisis (BGL 18.4, BP 165/95). Delaying intervention would have allowed continued physical exertion, exacerbating the active intraocular hemorrhage. The visit stabilized the patient hemodynamically, imposed strict physical restrictions, and facilitated an urgent, mandatory ophthalmology review to salvage the visual axis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate correction dose of fast-acting insulin as per patient's sliding scale. Generated urgent referral to the local tertiary ophthalmology clinic for B-scan ultrasound and potential panretinal photocoagulation or vitrectomy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To permanently enforce glycemic monitoring, the patient must place their glucometer directly on top of the television remote control. By physically blocking access to a high-frequency, desired activity (watching TV) until the blood sugar is checked, the patient is operantly conditioned to prioritize glucose testing, linking metabolic control directly to their daily routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral for community diabetic nursing education.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours for aggressive diabetes management and to confirm ophthalmology engagement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the vision goes completely black, if new flashing lights appear in the periphery, or if the eye becomes acutely painful and red.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis requires specialist confirmation via ultrasound due to the blood obscuring the retina.
Clinical Course: The blood in the eye may take weeks to months to clear naturally, or it may require surgery. Strict bed rest with the head elevated is required immediately to allow the blood to settle to the bottom of the eye.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not bend over, lift anything heavy, or lie completely flat. If you see flashing lights or a dark curtain descending over your vision, you must go to the emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The high sugar levels have damaged the blood vessels in your eye, causing abnormal, fragile new vessels to grow. One of these weak vessels has burst and bled into the jelly of your eye, blocking your vision.
Actionable Steps: Sleep with your head elevated on three pillows tonight to let gravity pull the blood down out of your line of sight. Strictly avoid coughing, straining, or heavy lifting. Check your blood sugar four times daily.

